<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Vorskla River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1399">1399</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Battle of the Vorskla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Vorskla River was fought on 12 August 1399 on the Vorskla, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> army of the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> and Khan Temür Qutlugh crushed Grand Duke Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, his Polish and Teutonic allies and the exiled khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>. Many Lithuanian princes were killed, and the defeat checked Lithuanian expansion into the steppe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river.jpg" alt="Battle of the Vorskla River" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.11,_p.421_-_Battle_at_Vorskla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1395, after losing his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, was dethroned by the party of Khan Temür Qutlugh and Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a>, who were supported by Timur. He fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and asked Vytautas for help in retaking the Horde, in exchange for surrendering his suzerainty over Ruthenian lands. Vytautas gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Moldavians and Wallachians, and signed the Treaty of Salynas, surrendering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. Expeditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> territory followed in 1397, 1398 and 1399. The first reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. In May 1399 Vytautas declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Tatars and received the blessing of Pope Boniface IX.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1399 the army moved against the Horde along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and on 5 August it met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> at the Vorskla, just north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a>. Temür Qutlugh proposed a three-day ceasefire, a trick to win time while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a>'s reinforcements arrived. Vytautas planned a great wagon fort to stop charging horsemen, which he would then destroy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. His army was well equipped but smaller. On 12 August Temür Qutlugh feigned a retreat, and Vytautas left the wagon fort to pursue him. Edigu's units appeared from behind and surrounded the Lithuanian army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> judged the battle lost and fled. The Tatars used their own artillery against the Lithuanian cavalry and captured the wagon fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Vytautas barely escaped alive. About 50 princes fought under his banners and about 20 of them were killed, among them his cousins Demetrius I Starszy and Andrei of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and Stephen I of Moldavia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, which paid a ransom, and pillaged as far west as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> in pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, who was assassinated in 1407 or 1408. The defeat blocked Lithuanian expansion into southern Ruthenia and cost the state its access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Smolensk, Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> revolted against Lithuanian rule.</p><p>Vytautas gave up his plans to break the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kreva">Kreva</a> and allied himself again with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It has been suggested that he learned the staged retreat during this battle and used it at Grunwald in 1410. The Lithuanian historian Edvardas Gudavičius judged that Vytautas took an unjustified risk and lost terribly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Vorskla River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even so, the emir Edigu crushed Vytautas of Lithuania at the <b>Battle of the Vorskla River</b> in 1399 and besieged Moscow in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vytautas suffered a heavy defeat against the Tatar commander Edigu at the <b>Battle of the Vorskla River</b> in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the Poles of Władysław II Jagiełło at the Battle of Grunwald, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li></ul></section>
